      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "Some studies reported an increased incidence of premature ventricular complexes (PVCs) during triggered myocardial contrast echocardiography (MCE) using high-intensity ultrasound destruction. Whether PVCs are also induced by real time MCE using low emission power, is unknown. The aim of the study was to assess the occurrence of arrhythmias during real time adenosine MCE in healthy volunteers and patients with stable coronary artery disease (CAD)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Fifty healthy volunteers and 26 patients with stable CAD underwent real time MCE using Sonovue and power pulse inversion (ATL 5000) at rest and during adenosine stress. The occurrence of premature atrial complexes (PAC) and PVCs was analyzed before and during MCE using ECG-tracings from videotapes."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In healthy subjects, the occurrence of PVCs at baseline (0.04 +/- 0.23 PVCs/min) was similar at rest (0.04 +/- 0.23 PVCs/min, P = NS), and adenosine stress (0.03 +/- 0.14, P = NS). In CAD patients, the occurrence of PVCs at baseline was 0.30 +/- 0.76 PVC/min, compared to 0.29 +/- 0.74 at rest (P = NS), and 0.34 +/- 0.74 during adenosine stress (P = NS). The number of subjects demonstrating PVCs did not increase during MCE. The occurrence of PACs during MCE was not increased compared to baseline."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Real time MCE using low emission power does not increase the occurrence of premature complexes in healthy volunteers or CAD patients."
        }
      ]},
